    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d670">

                <pb facs="1527" n="1489" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v04/pg1489"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">834.2614/8–1354</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Assistant Secretary of State for Economic
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kalijarvi</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Managing Director of the Export-Import Bank</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Edgerton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-08-13">August 13, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear General Edgerton</hi>: Reference is made to
                    your inquiry of August 10 as to the State Department’s position with regard to
                    the pending application of the Paraguayan Government for an Eximbank credit to
                    assist in financing the Asuncion Water Works Project.</p>
                <p>The Department supports consideration of this project by the Bank as indicated by
                    Mr. Corbett<anchor n="1" xml:id="d670-f1"/> at the Executive Committee Meeting
                    of July 8. While it is known that the Paraguayan Ambassador is urging immediate
                    action on his Government’s application, the Department is of the opinion that it
                    would be advantageous for United States-Paraguayan relations if the
                    authorization of this credit could follow <pb facs="1528" n="1490"
                        xml:id="pg1490"/> shortly after Ambassador Ageton, newly appointed United
                    States Ambassador to Paraguay, arrives at his post. Ambassador Ageton expects to
                    reach Asuncion about September 1.</p>
                <p>The Department would not object if for banking reasons the Eximbank wishes to
                    take action immediately on the application, provided that no announcement is
                    made until Ambassador Ageton informs the Paraguayan President of the Bank’s
                    action at the time he presents his credentials.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d670-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thorsten V. Kalijarvi</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d670-f1" n="1" xml:id="d670-f1-content">
                <p> Jack C. Corbett, Director, Office of Financial and Development Policy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d670-f2" n="2" xml:id="d670-f2-content">
                <p>On Sept. 2, 1954, the Export-Import Bank approved in principle a credit of up to
                    $7,200,000 in favor of the Republic of Paraguay to assist in financing the
                    purchase of U.S. services and equipment necessary for the installation of a
                    central water supply system in Asunción. For additional information, see
                    Export-Import Bank of Washington, <hi rendition="#italic">Nineteenth Semiannual
                        Report to Congress for the Period July–December 1954</hi> (Washington,
                    1955). pp. 13–14.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
